# ffmpeg — real-world examples

## Print file metadata (streams, codecs, duration)

```sh
ffmpeg -i path/to/file.mkv
```

## Convert all m4a files to mp3

```sh
for f in *.m4a; do ffmpeg -i "$f" -acodec libmp3lame -ab 320k "${f%.m4a}.mp3"; done
```

## Listen to 10 seconds of audio from a video file

```sh
# -ss = start time, -t = seconds to cut, -autoexit closes ffplay when done
ffmpeg -ss 00:34:24.85 -t 10 -i path/to/file.mp4 -f mp3 pipe:play | ffplay -i pipe:play -autoexit
```

## Remux mkv to mp4 without re-encoding — fast, helps Plex direct play

```sh
ffmpeg -i movie.mkv -c copy movie.mp4
```

## Transcode to 720p H.264 to shrink a file for the media server

```sh
ffmpeg -i input.mkv -vf scale=-2:720 -c:v libx264 -crf 23 -c:a copy output.mkv
```

## Extract the audio track only

```sh
ffmpeg -i episode.mp4 -vn -acodec copy episode-audio.aac
```

## Grab a single frame as a poster/thumbnail

```sh
ffmpeg -ss 00:05:00 -i movie.mkv -frames:v 1 poster.jpg
```

| Flag | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| -i | input file |
| -c copy | copy streams, no re-encode |
| -c:v / -c:a | video / audio codec |
| -ss | seek to start time |
| -t | duration to process |
| -vf | video filter (scale, crop, ...) |
| -vn / -an | drop video / drop audio |
| -crf | x264 quality: lower = better, 18-28 typical |
